Requirements
------------

- Qt 6.8 or later, with the Qt Qml and Qt HTTP Server modules
- zlib

Build and run
-------------

$ git clone https://github.com/qt-users-jp/silk.git
$ cd silk
$ qmake
$ make
$ ./bin/silk
silk is running on 8080

To listen on a UNIX domain socket instead of a TCP port (e.g. behind a reverse proxy),
pass --listen.socket or set it in silkconfig.qml:

$ ./bin/silk --listen.socket /run/silk/silk.sock

    listen: Listen {
        socket: '/run/silk/silk.sock'
        socketAccess: 'group'   // user (default), group or world
    }

Only the owner of the silk process may connect to the socket by default. Use 'group'
and run the reverse proxy in silk's group, or --listen.socketAccess group.

Behind a reverse proxy
----------------------

silk takes the client address (http.remoteAddress) from the last entry of
X-Forwarded-For, only when the connection comes from a trusted proxy. X-Real-IP is
not used. Loopback and UNIX domain socket peers are always trusted. List other
proxies, e.g. the gateway of a Docker network, as addresses or subnets:

    property var proxy: {
        'trusted': ['172.17.0.1', '10.0.0.0/8']
    }

Every client that reaches silk through a trusted address can set its own client
address. With Docker, connections to a published port arrive from the gateway, so
publish the port on 127.0.0.1 only (e.g. -p 127.0.0.1:8008:8080) when trusting it.

The proxy must append the client address to X-Forwarded-For, and pass the Host header
if pages use http.host. With nginx:

    location / {
        proxy_pass http://unix:/run/silk/silk.sock;
        proxy_set_header Host $host;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
    }

Redirects to directories use a relative Location, so the proxy does not need to
rewrite it.

WebSocket (Silk.WebSocket) needs a TCP listener: Qt WebSockets cannot use a UNIX domain
socket, and upgrades arriving there are answered with 501. Behind nginx, forward the
upgrade to a TCP address:

    location /chat/ {
        proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:8080;
        proxy_http_version 1.1;
        proxy_set_header Upgrade $http_upgrade;
        proxy_set_header Connection "upgrade";
        proxy_set_header Host $host;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
    }

Demos
-----

The demos are built with silk, but not installed. Run them from the build directory.

- minimal: silk embedded in an application, with its pages and configuration
  (minimalconfig.qml) in the resources. Copy demos/minimal/ and rename minimal* to
  start your own service.

  $ ./bin/minimal
  (http://localhost:8080/)

- carpet: a backup service with its own QML import (Carpet). It backs up your home
  directory to ~/.carpet. The settings page starts a backup, turns on backups every
  30 minutes (off at first) and takes patterns of files not to back up. Showing
  changes needs the diff command.
  To back up something else, copy demos/carpet/src/carpet/carpetconfig.qml, set
  carpet: { 'from': ..., 'to': ... } in it and pass it with --config.

  $ ./bin/carpet
  (http://localhost:18080/)

- bootstrap: part of the Bootstrap 2.3 documentation written in QML (Silk.Bootstrap),
  served by silk itself. Only some pages were ported; links to the others end in 404.
  CSS and JavaScript are loaded from getbootstrap.com.

  $ ./bin/silk --config demos/bootstrapconfig.qml
  (http://localhost:8081/)

Changes in Qt 6 that affect silk applications
---------------------------------------------

- XMLHttpRequest no longer reads local files by default. Run silk with the
  environment variable QML_XHR_ALLOW_FILE_READ=1 if your QML pages need it.
- JavaScript files are served as text/javascript instead of application/javascript.
  Use the new name in the deflate configuration if you listed the old one.
- Response header names are sent in lower case. HTTP header names are case-insensitive.
- Injecting signal parameters into handlers, e.g. onMessage: console.log(message),
  is deprecated and prints a warning. Declare them instead:
  onMessage: function(message) { console.log(message) }
- WebSocket is implemented with Qt WebSockets (RFC 6455). The obsolete hixie-76
  handshake (draft-00) of 2010 browsers is no longer supported.
- A WebSocket upgrade must be the first request on its connection, as browsers send it.
- A rejected WebSocket upgrade (e.g. a missing page) gets an HTTP error response
  instead of a closed connection. WebSocket.close() sends a close frame.
- A text message arrives as a string in message.data, a binary one as an ArrayBuffer.
  send() sends text.
- A WebSocket message may be up to 1 MiB; a larger one closes the connection. Raise the
  limit in silkconfig.qml if pages need it:

      property var websocket: {
          'maxMessageSize': 16777216
      }
